`StatusByTypeId` is already a map keyed by `typeId`, so the change-detection entry can be retrieved in O(1) with a direct property access instead of an O(n) `Object.values(...).filter(...)` scan:
```ts
const changeStatus = statuses[CHANGE_DETECTION_STATUS_TYPE_ID]?.value ?? 'status-value:unknown';
const testValues = Object.values(statuses)
  .filter(s => s.typeId !== CHANGE_DETECTION_STATUS_TYPE_ID)
  .map(s => s.value);
return { changeStatus, testStatus: getMostCriticalStatusValue(testValues) };
```
This also eliminates the redundant `getMostCriticalStatusValue` call on the change slot when there is at most one change-detection entry per story.

The logic that splits statuses into `changeValues` / `testValues` by `typeId` is duplicated verbatim here and in `getChangeDetectionStatus` in `Tree.tsx`. Once `getChangeDetectionStatus` is moved to `status.tsx` (see general comment), `getGroupDualStatus` can call it per-leaf to eliminate this duplication:
```ts
const leafStatuses = leafs.map(story => getChangeDetectionStatus(allStatuses[story.id!] || {}));
const changeValues = leafStatuses.map(s => s.changeStatus);
const testValues   = leafStatuses.map(s => s.testStatus);
```

`BranchWithChangeDetectionPriority` is first constructed via `makeDualSlotStory(...)` (which sets a `render` function) and then immediately mutated with `BranchWithChangeDetectionPriority.render = ...`, silently overwriting what the factory produced. This is surprising to readers who expect the exported value to be fully described inline. Consider accepting the selected ID as a parameter to `makeDualSlotStory`, or using object spread at the declaration site:
```ts
export const BranchWithChangeDetectionPriority: Story = {
  ...makeDualSlotStory({ ... }),
  render: (args) => { ... },
};
```
